        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 树",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "树 — shù — \"tree\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "秋天的树最好看 qiūtiān de shù zuì hǎokàn — Trees look their best in autumn."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The new character, in pieces: 树 (shù) — nine strokes in three pieces: on the left the tree piece, four strokes, as in 机; in the middle 又, two strokes, the second shrunk to a short stroke; and on the right a bar, a vertical that hooks to the left, and a dot. The next lesson puts it in your hand."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "树 (shù) is inherited from Middle Chinese dzyuH (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), glossed as the general name for trees and 'to set up'; the dictionary also lists dzyuX, glossed 'to prop up'. The falling tone of shù comes from dzyuH."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for saying how it was."
